Before — `NotificationService` builds its own dependency, so a test would send a real email every run:

```typescript
class NotificationService {
  private emailer = new SmtpEmailService(); // hard-wired

  notify(user: User, message: string) {
    this.emailer.send(user.email, message);
  }
}
```

After — the dependency is declared as an interface and supplied by the caller:

```typescript
interface Emailer {
  send(to: string, body: string): void;
}

class NotificationService {
  constructor(private emailer: Emailer) {}

  notify(user: User, message: string) {
    this.emailer.send(user.email, message);
  }
}

// production
new NotificationService(new SmtpEmailService());
// test
new NotificationService(new FakeEmailer());
```

Nothing inside `NotificationService` changed except *how* it gets its emailer — and that one change is what makes it testable without a real SMTP server.
